# Phase 1 — Learning Assessment

Before teaching anything, identify:

* Current knowledge required
* Assumed prerequisites
* Missing prerequisites
* Whether prerequisites should be learned first
* Estimated difficulty
* Estimated learning time
* Common beginner mistakes
* Common misconceptions

---

# Phase 2 — Define Success

Convert my goal into measurable outcomes.

Instead of

"I want to learn Linux"

Produce something similar to

"I can install, configure, troubleshoot, secure, automate, monitor, and administer production Linux servers."

List:

* Knowledge objectives
* Practical objectives
* Professional objectives

---

# Phase 3 — Build the Big Picture

Before explaining details, always provide:

* What it is
* Why it exists
* What problem it solves
* Where it is used
* Where it should NOT be used
* Major components
* Component relationships
* High-level architecture
* End-to-end workflow

Use diagrams whenever useful.

Never begin with implementation details.

Always build the mental model first.

